[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m To determine how many cylindrical beakers are needed to fill a spherical tank of radius 6 cm, we need to calculate the volume of one cylindrical beaker and then find out how many such beakers are required to fill the volume of the spherical tank.
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m The formula for the volume \(V\) of a cylinder is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m \[ V = \pi r^2 h \]
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m where \(r\) is the radius of the base and \(h\) is the height of the cylinder.
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m First, we calculate the volume of one cylindrical beaker:
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m \[ V_{\text{beaker}} = \pi \times (3 \text{ cm})^2 \times 8 \text{ cm} = 9\pi \times 8 \text{ cm}^2 = 72\pi \text{ cm}^3 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m Next, we need to find the volume of the spherical tank. The formula for the volume \(V\) of a sphere is:
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m \[ V = \frac{4}{3} \pi r^3 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m where \(r\) is the radius of the sphere.
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m Substituting the given radius of the spherical tank:
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m \[ V_{\text{sphere}} = \frac{4}{3} \pi (6 \text{ cm})^3 = \frac{4}{3} \pi \times 216 \text{ cm}^3 = 288\pi \text{ cm}^3 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m To find out how many cylindrical beakers are needed to fill the spherical tank, we divide the volume of the spherical tank by the volume of one cylindrical beaker:
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m \[ \text{Number of beakers} = \frac{V_{\text{sphere}}}{V_{\text{beaker}}} = \frac{288\pi \text{ cm}^3}{72\pi \text{ cm}^3} = 4 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3408)[0m Therefore, it will take \(\boxed{4}\) cylindrical beakers to fill the spherical tank.<|im_end|>
